import
	../html
	../page-template

index html()
	page-template "index", "Keen programming language",
		main: index-main
-index-main html[]()
	- keen-runnable quote
		# I'm editable!
		main void()
			for x in 1 .. 21
				m3 = is-multiple-of x, 3
				m5 = is-multiple-of x, 5
				log if m3 and m5
					"FizzBuzz"
				elif m3
					"Fizz"
				elif m5
					"Buzz"
				else
					"\{x\}"
	- aside-small "Hover over things above for an explanation."
	- p "{strong "Keen"} is a general-purpose programming language."
	- p "Some interesting features:"
	- bullets
		- "Keen programs can be run directly with no build step, or built to a JAR or JS file."
		- quote
			Everything is an expression, including {if_} and {for_}.
			Expressions are nested using indentation.
		- quote
			The meaning of an expression depends on the expected type.
			{br}
			The same syntax {num-1-2}
			could be an array, tuple, vector, etc., based on where it is used.
		- "Everything goes in modules; there are no globally visible declarations."
		- quote
			Keen is strongly typed, with no subtypes or {null}s.
			{br}
			It does have some safe implicit conversions (such as {nat32} to {nat64}), but no user-defined ones.
		- "Global state is restricted. Usually side effects are done through interfaces."
		- quote
			The type system tracks mutability and prevents sharing non-thread-safe mutable data across threads.
			{br}
			But if you just want to write a single-threaded program with mutable data, it won't get in your way.
		- "{intro-link specs} allow you to call functions provided by the caller's context."
		- "{intro-link contexts} provide an alternative for repetitive parameters."
	- p (style: "font-size: 125%"), "To get started, read the {intro-link}."

-intro-link html-node()
	link "/intro", ("intro",)
